	abstract = {Understanding entrepreneurial ecosystem perceptions across countries is essential for explaining venture creation and regional development. However, comparative entrepreneurship research lacks a comprehensive framework for international analysis. This study proposes an institutional framework using Global Entrepreneurship Monitor data that evaluates participatory, regulatory, supportive, and cognitive institutions. The framework is applied to compare two emerging economies, Mexico and Brazil. Results indicate that Mexico exhibits stronger institutions across all four dimensions. The proposed approach offers academics and practitioners a practical tool for assessing entrepreneurial ecosystems and designing policies that support new business formation and sustainable entrepreneurial development. © 2026 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.},
