	abstract = {The article aims to analyze the portrayal of the prostitute protagonists in the novels Tereza Batista cansada de guerra (1972) and Tieta do Agreste (1977) by the Bahian writer Jorge Amado. Two important features can be observed in the narratives, characteristic of the second phase of Amado’s work: the presence of a strong female figure as the protagonist and the character’s ability to mediate the everyday lives and challenges of marginalized social classes in Brazil. The article first examines how prostitution is represented in the lives of the protagonists, seeking to determine whether it functions solely as a means of subsistence or whether it carries other meanings. It then explores the theme of female emancipation, which is pursued and achieved by both protagonists. Prostitution, as a path to independence, allows them to break free from patriarchal social structures and to take control of their own destinies. This positive impact is reflected in the development of the protagonists’ key characteristics, such as entrepreneurship, resilience, combativeness, and leadership. At the same time, attention is drawn to the persistence of prejudice associated with this profession and to the adverse living conditions of this social group, marked by violence and exploitation. © 2025 University of Ostrava. All rights reserved.},
