	abstract = {This article examines the short-term effects of Brazil’s Economic Freedom Law (Law No. 13,874/2019) on formal labor market dynamics. The legislation eliminated prior authorization requirements for low-risk business activities, aiming to reduce bureaucratic barriers to entrepreneurship. The analysis uses monthly administrative data from 2017 to 2019 and a rigorous difference-in-differences approach to estimate the law’s impact on hiring, dismissals, and net employment. Results indicate an average increase of 1.62 hires and a statistically significant net gain of 2.44 jobs per low-risk activity per month after implementation. The effects are strongest in the commerce and services sectors and in the Southeast region, where net job creation reached up to 12 new positions per activity. Robustness checks based on pre-intervention trends support the validity of the findings. The study contributes to international policy debates by providing evidence on how regulatory simplification can influence employment outcomes in a large developing economy. © 2025 The Korean Association for Public Administration.},
