	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105009468631?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Faculty of Health Sciences, Mersin University, Mersin, Turkey},
	abstract = {The rise of kidpreneurship -children engaging in entrepreneurial activities- reflects a profound shift in how societies view childhood, opportunity, and economic participation. Particularly in developing countries, kidpreneurship offers both promising prospects for empowerment and significant ethical concerns regarding exploitation and education disruption. This study examines the ethical dilemmas, legal frameworks, and socio-cultural dynamics surrounding kidpreneurship in Turkiye, India, Indonesia, Brazil, and South Africa. A mixed-methods comparative analysis was conducted, utilizing global datasets on child labor and entrepreneurial ecosystem indicators. Results from clustering analyses reveal three distinct patterns: Turkiye and India cluster together, Indonesia and Brazil form another group, while South Africa stands independently with unique dynamics. Socio-economic pressures, legal gaps, and cultural norms critically influence children’s entrepreneurial experiences across these regions. The findings emphasize the urgent need for integrated policies that protect children’s rights while fostering ethical entrepreneurial development. By analyzing the interaction between international conventions and local legislation, this study offers policy recommendations aimed at promoting empowerment without exploitation. This research contributes to the emerging literature on youth entrepreneurship ethics and proposes strategies to create sustainable, child-centered entrepreneurial ecosystems. © 2025 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.},
