	abstract = {In this scope, we address issues around political networks, focusing on public-private partnerships in/for the implementation process of the Common Core in Brazil (BNCC), especially regarding discursive productions on teacher training and curriculum production.In the midst of the convergence of governance and educational entrepreneurship, we ask: who really owns public education? We investigated how tools and support materials available in digital media are presented to the PROBNCC teams responsible for teacher training and curriculum production in/for schools as a necessary way to “enforce the BNCC, in practice”. We present data from qualitative research developed in the cities of Mossoró/RN and Rio de Janeiro/RJ on the BNCC implementation process. In the state/municipal collaboration regime, the focus on continuing education for teachers has been strategic for the “implementation” of the Common Core in an attempt to control a mode of curricular organization. We argue that schools, as a public space for creation, build their policies all the time, regardless of the forms used by state and municipal education departments. © 2022 Curriculo sem Fronteiras. All rights reserved.},
