	abstract = {An important problem in Multi-Criteria Decision Analysis arises when one must select at least two alternatives at the same time. This can be denoted as a multiple choice problem. In other words, instead of evaluating each of the alternatives separately, they must be combined into groups of n alternatives, where n = 2. When the multiple choice problem must be solved under multiple criteria, the result is a multi-criteria, multiple choice problem. In this paper, it is shown through examples how this problem can be tackled on a bipolar scale. The Choquet integral is used in this paper to take care of interactions between criteria. A numerical application example is conducted using data from SEBRAE-RJ, a nonprofit private organization that has the mission of promoting competitiveness, sustainable development and entrepreneurship in the state of Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. The paper closes with suggestions for future research. © 2015 Brazilian Operations Research Society.},
